# Command myclaude

## What does this command do:

Launches `claude` inside a named `abduco` session, with `script(1)` recording
the entire terminal session to disk. On exit it cleans the raw `.log` into a
readable `.txt` sibling by stripping alt-screen redraws and ANSI escapes (via
`ansifilter`/`ansi2txt`). The older `screen`-based implementation ships
alongside it as `myclaude-screen` for hosts without `abduco`.

## Installed from

For Fedora install using: git clone git@github.com:kevinpinscoe/tools.git ~/tools (then keep ~/tools on PATH)
For Raspberry Pi install using: git clone git@github.com:kevinpinscoe/tools.git ~/tools (then keep ~/tools on PATH)
For Mac install using: git clone git@github.com:kevinpinscoe/tools.git ~/tools (then keep ~/tools on PATH)
For Mac container (Fedora Linux, ARM/aarch64) install using: git clone git@github.com:kevinpinscoe/tools.git ~/tools (then keep ~/tools on PATH)

> Not a package — it's a script in the `kevinpinscoe/tools` repo. Pull to sync
> updates: `git -C ~/tools pull`.

## Command path

For Fedora run: ~/tools/myclaude
For Raspberry Pi run: ~/tools/myclaude
For Mac run: ~/tools/myclaude
For Mac container run: ~/tools/myclaude

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at ~/tools/RUNBOOK.md (## myclaude section)

## Command options

# Launch (run from any directory under $HOME; prompts for a session name)
myclaude

# Post-process a raw .log into a cleaned .txt sibling (e.g. after a detach)
myclaude --clean <log-file>

# Detach from a running session (session keeps running and logging)
# Ctrl+\

# Reattach to a detached session
abduco -a <session-name>

# List running sessions
abduco

# Legacy screen-based variant, for hosts without abduco
myclaude-screen
